UPDATE "gaps" SET "description" = 'TERM ADJUDICATION HAS NEVER RUN, AND NOTHING SAYS SO. Harvest at evidence, adjudicate at judgment (owner 2026-08-27, overruling DR-2026-08-24 section 2.4). The harvest half works -- derive it with: select count(*) from observed_terms. The adjudication half has produced nothing at all -- select count(*) from term_adjudications -- and db.py adjudicate-term exists, so this is a backlog, not a missing capability. CLAUDE.md section 6 says outright that zero links AFTER judgment is a defect, and no gap, no check and none of the five documents a cold-starting session reads names this. Found by walking the spine forward from this batch''s two admissions: research -> evidence walks, evidence -> judgment stops here with no marker. WHAT WOULD CLOSE IT: adjudicate the harvested terms, or record why the backlog is deliberate. WHAT WOULD MAKE IT VISIBLE MEANWHILE: a check asserting that observed_terms older than N batches have an adjudication, which is the general form of the same question. || CORRECTED 2026-09-25: BATCH 20: ADJUDICATION HAS RUN OVER THE WHOLE BACKLOG, AND THIS GAP STAYS OPEN ONLY FOR WHAT WAS DEFERRED WITH A REASON. Every observed_terms row now carries a term_adjudications row, each with an observation-specific rationale. DERIVE THE COUNTS, DO NOT QUOTE THEM: select outcome, count(*) from term_adjudications group by outcome; and the residual backlog: select count(*) from observed_terms o where not exists (select 1 from term_adjudications a where a.observation_id=o.observation_id). SIX TERMS WERE MINTED through add-term (NAMES-NEW), each warranted by the corpus rather than coined: ramp (the element; TERM-001 is its slope), level difference, ramp run length, intermediate landing, ramp usability (the outcome side: completion, Borg effort, discomfort and difficulty ratings are adjudicated to it as measures), and accessibility audit -- derive them with select term_id, canonical_en from terms where created_by_session like ''%batch-20