UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "notes" = 'SURFACED WHILE FAILING TO FIND SOMETHING ELSE, which is the honest provenance. Crossref, queried on the dissertation title REF-01005''s bibliography carries, returned John A. Templer''s indexed corpus instead: this 1978 NBS report, ''The Forgiving Stair'' (1984, Human Factors Society, doi 10.1177/154193128402800118), and ''The Soft Stair: Falls Induced by a Laboratory Stair'' (1989, doi 10.1518/107118189786758148). Full author list here: John A Templer, Gary M Mullet, John Archea, Stephen T Margulis. WHAT MAKES IT CHEAP TO RESOLVE: NBS is now NIST and its internal reports are public, so unlike Walter 1971 and Elmer 1957 this one is very likely retrievable in full without document delivery -- nvlpubs.nist.gov is the route and it has not been tried. IF RETRIEVED, THE QUESTION TO ASK IS NARROW: does it contain any rampway slope or length recommendation at all? If yes, footnote c probably means this and the 1:8/1:10 warrant is identified. If no -- which is what its title predicts -- then footnote c''s Templer is the 1974 dissertation or an item outside every index reached, and either way the warrant for those two bands rests on stair research. THAT QUESTION IS WORTH ONE RETRIEVAL AND NOT MORE. || RESOLVED 2026-09-20 (R15, re-described from the source): R15 CORRECTION: THE PREMISE FOR STAGING THIS ROW IS GONE. It was staged as "the nearest RESOLVABLE item to Table 13 footnote c" on the reasoning that John A. Templer had no ramp research and that a 1978 stairs report was therefore the closest thing to the cited 1977 item. That reasoning is retracted on exec 90: Templer FHWA-RD-79-1/-2/-3 is dedicated ramp research and is candidate 125. THIS ROW IS NOW WHAT IT ALWAYS WAS ON ITS FACE -- a study of STAIR users, off parameter 3, with no claim to make about ramp gradient. Keep it PENDING rather than retiring it: NBS IR 78-1554 is also free on Internet Archive (identifier analysisofbehavi7815temp), it is by the same author in the same period, and if the FHWA trail fails to identify footnote c referent this is still a node worth reading. But it is no longer the first call, and the question this row was staged to answer should be put to candidate 125 instead. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): REHOME TO stair-ramp-threshold-biomechanics-accessibility (an ACTIVE slug in the slugs table), NOT ADMITTED ON PARAMETER 3. Retrieved in full from NIST (nbsir78-1554.pdf, persisted) and read -- the text layer is sound, and the low-text pages were checked. It is what its title says and what this row''s own staging note predicted: a behavioural and accident study of STAIR users for the Consumer Product Safety Commission, with performance statements on risers, treads, handrails, lighting and visual orientation. It contains NO RAMP SLOPE OR LENGTH RECOMMENDATION: every ''ramp'' is incidental (scope, alternatives to stairs, not mistaking a stair for a ramp, falls on stairs versus falls on ramps, and citations), and every ''gradient'' is the report''s ''orientation gradient'', a perceptual measure, not a slope. R15 CORRECTIONS: the staged title gives 1978; the title page reads ''February 1976, Issued November 1978'' -- the report was written two years before it was issued. The staging premise (nearest resolvable item to footnote c''s ''Templer, 1977'') was already retracted on 2026-09-20 in favour of candidate 125, now REF-01008. WHAT THE READ ADDS TO THE FOOTNOTE-c QUESTION, WITHOUT ANSWERING IT: the report cites ''Templer, J., Stairs and Ramps, Dowden, Hutchinson and Ross, Stroudsburg, PA., forthcoming'' six times -- a Templer book on ramps announced in a 1976-78 federal report. That is a third possible referent for ''Templer, 1977'' beside the 1974 dissertation and the FHWA ramp study, and it is NOT chosen here (candidate 115 stays PENDING). Rehome, not out-of-scope: stair-user behaviour, risers, treads and handrails are that slug''s subject, and a later pass there should read it as primary stair evidence. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source): CORRECTION TO THE RE-DESCRIPTION ABOVE, SAME BATCH. It says ''the report was written two years before it was issued''. The title page prints ''February 1976'' and ''Issued November 1978''; it does not say what the earlier date marks (drafting, contract completion, a period covered). Recorded as printed, without the inference. Everything else in the rehoming stands.' WHERE "candidate_id" = 117;
UPDATE "search_candidates" SET "notes" = 'harm_finding=1 on the same reading as its sibling -- the title asks whether people are being DENIED a right. RETRIEVAL IS CHEAP AND THE ROUTE IS KNOWN, which is what separates this cluster from everything else this batch chased: Walter 1971, Elmer 1957 and Templer are all behind GAP-041''s closed catalogue stratum, while these are DOI-bearing open-access articles from the deposit-based indexes that DO work here. A batch that wants measured ramp gradients rather than archival provenance should start here. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source; admitted as REF-01007): ADMITTED AS REF-01007, T3 (clinical, lower_control), AND TWO THINGS THE STAGED ROW SAID ARE CORRECTED FROM THE SOURCE. Read in full from the Europe PMC JATS XML (PMC7999795, persisted as retrieval-log/<session>/2c65a16afca1967b.xml); bibliographic fields from the Crossref payload. (1) THE TIER GUESS WAS WRONG. The row guessed tier 2 because ''a national facility audit is closer to systematic evidence than to grey primary''. It is neither: it is a SECONDARY ANALYSIS of a cross-sectional census (PMAQ-AB first cycle, 2012) of 38,812 public primary health care facilities, which the ladder places at T3 clinical lower_control. Scale does not make a census a synthesis. (2) THE HOPE THAT IT ''TABULATES MEASURED GRADIENTS'' IS FALSE, AND THE SOURCE SAYS WHY. The instrument is 22 yes/no items; the ramp item is ''Does the health facility have access ramp?'' -- presence only, no gradient. The authors give the reason in their introduction: tools that require ''measurement of slopes of ramps and widths of doors'' are complex, ''As a consequence, few large-scale accessibility audits have been conducted''. So the one national audit in this cluster bought its scale by not measuring gradient. Filed on parameter 3 as claim_type ABSENT, which is the honest reading and is itself a finding about the audit literature. WHAT STANDS FROM THE STAGED ROW: harm_finding=1 -- access ramp present at 44 percent of facilities, handrail at the entrance 8 percent, accessible entrance 35 percent, all worse in the poorer North/Northeast and in small municipalities; the authors call it a violation of the right to health under the UNCRPD and Brazilian law. R13 graded PROXY: nobody disabled took part in the measurement in any role. NOT Co-1 on any reading. Backward pass run on admission (exec 95); it staged candidates 129 and 130. || RESOLVED 2026-09-25 (R15, re-described from the source; admitted as REF-01007): CORRECTION TO THE RE-DESCRIPTION ABOVE, SAME BATCH. It says ''The authors give the reason in their introduction'' and that ''the one national audit in this cluster bought its scale by not measuring gradient''. Over-read: the introduction explains why large audits are rare in general (slope and width measurement make tools complex), and the paper is a secondary analysis of a Ministry of Health census whose instrument its authors did not design; no sentence says why that instrument asks about ramp presence and not gradient. Presence-only and absent on parameter 3 stand; the causal claim is withdrawn. Extraction 59 carries the same correction.' WHERE "candidate_id" = 124;
